  "prediction": "The report outlines the constitutional process for overriding a presidential veto in both the House of Representatives and the Senate. When the President vetoes a bill, Congress has the power to override the veto with a two-thirds vote in both chambers. In the House, the Speaker of the House reads the veto message and debates on overriding the veto take place under the rules of the House. Debate is limited to one hour, and a roll-call vote is taken to override the veto. If the override fails, the bill is typically referred back to committee. In the Senate, the process is similar, but the Senate may take more time to consider the vetoed bill. The Senate may also limit debate through unanimous consent or cloture. Once a two-thirds vote is achieved in the Senate, the bill becomes law. If the House and Senate both override a veto, the bill becomes law. If only one chamber overrides the veto, the process ends there. The report also notes that the Constitution requires a roll-call vote and that a motion to reconsider the override vote is only in order if the override fails.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the constitutional process for overriding a presidential veto in both the House of Representatives and the Senate. When the President vetoes a bill, Congress has the power to override the veto with a two-thirds vote in both chambers. In the House, the Speaker of the House reads the veto message and debates on overriding the veto take place under the rules of the House. Debate is limited to one hour, and a roll-call vote is taken to override the veto. If the override fails, the bill is typically referred back to committee. In the Senate, the process is similar, but the Senate may take more time to consider the vetoed bill. The Senate may also limit debate through unanimous consent or cloture. Once a two-thirds vote is achieved in the Senate, the bill becomes law. If the House and Senate both override a veto, the bill becomes law. If only one chamber overrides the veto, the process ends there. The report also notes that the Constitution requires a roll-call vote and that a motion to reconsider the override vote is only in order if the override fails.",
  "score": 31.638417579495044,
  "answers": [
    "A bill or joint resolution that has been vetoed by the President can become law if two-thirds of the Members voting in the House and the Senate each agree to pass it over the President's objection. The chambers act sequentially on vetoed measures: The House acts first on House-originated measures (H.R. and H.J. Res.), and the Senate acts first on Senate-originated measures (S. and S.J. Res.). If the first-acting chamber fails to override the veto, the other chamber cannot consider it. The House typically considers the question of overriding a presidential veto under the hour rule, with time customarily controlled and allocated by the chair and ranking member of the committee with jurisdiction over the bill. The Senate usually considers the question of overriding a veto under the terms of a unanimous consent agreement."
  ],
